      <h2>Indian Transport Rules & Regulations</h2>
      <p>
        Stay compliant with Indian transport laws to avoid penalties and ensure smooth operations. 
        This guide covers essential regulations for commercial vehicle operators.
      </p>

      <h3>Motor Vehicles Act, 1988</h3>
      <p>
        The Motor Vehicles Act is the primary legislation governing road transport in India. 
        Key provisions include licensing, registration, vehicle standards, and traffic rules.
      </p>

      <h3>Commercial Vehicle Permits</h3>
      <p>Commercial vehicles require appropriate permits based on their operation:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>All India Permit:</strong> Valid across India for goods transport</li>
        <li><strong>National Permit:</strong> For goods vehicles to operate in multiple states</li>
        <li><strong>State Permit:</strong> Limited to operations within a specific state</li>
        <li><strong>Contract Carriage Permit:</strong> For passenger vehicles under contract</li>
        <li><strong>Stage Carriage Permit:</strong> For buses operating on fixed routes</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Driver Requirements</h3>
      <div class="highlight-box">
        <p><strong>Commercial drivers must possess:</strong></p>
        <ul style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
          <li>Valid Commercial Driving License (CDL) for the vehicle category</li>
          <li>Badge issued by the Regional Transport Office (RTO)</li>
          <li>Medical fitness certificate</li>
          <li>Training certificate for hazardous goods (if applicable)</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h3>Vehicle Documentation</h3>
      <p>Commercial vehicles must carry the following documents:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Registration Certificate (RC)</li>
        <li>Valid insurance policy</li>
        <li>Pollution Under Control (PUC) certificate</li>
        <li>Fitness certificate</li>
        <li>Permit (as applicable)</li>
        <li>Tax receipt</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Weight and Load Limits</h3>
      <p>
        Overloading is a serious offense. Ensure compliance with Gross Vehicle Weight (GVW) limits 
        specified on the vehicle's registration certificate. Penalties for overloading can be severe.
      </p>

      <h3>Mandatory Safety Features</h3>
      <p>Commercial vehicles must be equipped with:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>GPS tracking system (AIS-140 compliant)</li>
        <li>Speed governors (for vehicles >12 tons GVW)</li>
        <li>Reflective tapes</li>
        <li>First aid box</li>
        <li>Fire extinguisher</li>
        <li>Emergency contact information</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Working Hours & Rest Periods</h3>
      <p>
        The Motor Transport Workers Act, 1961 regulates working hours for drivers. Maximum driving 
        hours are typically 8-10 hours per day with mandatory rest periods to prevent fatigue.
      </p>

      <h3>E-Way Bill Requirements</h3>
      <div class="highlight-box">
        <p><strong>E-Way Bill is mandatory for:</strong></p>
        <ul style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
          <li>Goods valued over ₹50,000</li>
          <li>Inter-state movement of goods</li>
          <li>Intra-state movement (in most states)</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">
          Generate e-Way Bills through the GST portal before transporting goods to avoid penalties.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3>Penalties and Violations</h3>
      <p>Common violations and their penalties:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Driving without license: ₹5,000 fine and/or imprisonment</li>
        <li>Overloading: ₹20,000 per extra ton</li>
        <li>No insurance: ₹2,000 (first offense), ₹4,000 (subsequent)</li>
        <li>Overriding speed limit: ₹1,000-2,000</li>
        <li>No PUC certificate: ₹10,000</li>
      </ul>
